WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.259
Remember Wren? Wren found a cave full of pentaja...

00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:07.775
and the money of a whole village stopped working.

00:00:08.125 --> 00:00:13.836
Wren is made up. But again and again in history, money has had a Wren.

00:00:13.836 --> 00:00:18.324
A stone wheel. A glass bead. A seashell. A silver coin.

00:00:18.324 --> 00:00:23.056
Each time, someone found a cheaper way to make more of it.

00:00:23.407 --> 00:00:26.223
This is: Every Wren in History.

00:00:28.172 --> 00:00:32.535
Our first stop is Yap, a small island in the western Pacific.

00:00:32.535 --> 00:00:35.325
Yap has no good stone for making money.

00:00:35.325 --> 00:00:38.972
So for generations, Yapese sailors crossed hundreds

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:42.763
of kilometres of open ocean, to the islands of Palau.

00:00:43.113 --> 00:00:45.438
There, with tools made of shell,

00:00:45.438 --> 00:00:50.233
they carved limestone into great wheels with a hole in the middle.

00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:55.318
They called them rai. Then they hauled them home, on canoes and rafts.

00:00:55.318 --> 00:00:57.207
Not everyone made it back.

00:00:57.557 --> 00:01:01.932
Every rai cost a dangerous voyage, so there were never many.

00:01:01.932 --> 00:01:04.193
That's what made them precious:

00:01:04.193 --> 00:01:09.297
their size, the whiteness of the stone, and how few ever made it home.

00:01:09.297 --> 00:01:14.475
A stone about 2 feet across could buy a big pig, or 50 baskets of food.

00:01:14.825 --> 00:01:19.476
The biggest stones were far too heavy to move. So nobody moved them.

00:01:19.476 --> 00:01:23.990
When one changed hands, everyone just remembered who owned it now.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:27.957
One family's fortune was a stone at the bottom of the sea,

00:01:27.957 --> 00:01:32.471
lost in a storm generations earlier. The village still counted it.

00:01:32.822 --> 00:01:35.763
Then, in 1871, an Irish-American captain

00:01:35.763 --> 00:01:38.998
called David O'Keefe was shipwrecked on Yap.

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:42.527
He came back with a sailing ship and iron tools,

00:01:42.527 --> 00:01:44.880
and made the islanders an offer:

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.394
he would bring their stones home from Palau, in exchange for dried coconut.

00:01:50.745 --> 00:01:54.332
Stones that had been rare in 1840 became common.

00:01:54.332 --> 00:01:58.143
By 1929, there were more than 13,000 on the island.

00:01:58.143 --> 00:02:00.534
The biggest were 12 feet across.

00:02:00.885 --> 00:02:04.582
Sound familiar? It's Wren's cave, all over again.

00:02:04.582 --> 00:02:10.165
But this time, the islanders noticed. By later accounts, O'Keefe's stones,

00:02:10.165 --> 00:02:15.522
easy to get and suddenly everywhere, traded for less than the old ones.

00:02:15.522 --> 00:02:17.409
Yap had spotted its Wren.

00:02:17.758 --> 00:02:20.910
On Yap, everyone knew each stone's story,

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:25.907
so new stones could be priced lower, and old savings stayed safe.

00:02:25.907 --> 00:02:28.829
In Ulm Hollow, a pentaja is a pentaja:

00:02:28.829 --> 00:02:34.441
Wren's cave made everyone's pentaja worth less. Yap's money had a memory.

00:02:34.441 --> 00:02:35.901
Most money doesn't.

